# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# !! READ THIS FIRST — EVERY VITE_ VARIABLE IS PUBLIC !!
#
# Vite statically inlines every variable prefixed with VITE_ into the client
# JavaScript at build time. That means the value ships to every visitor and
# can be read from browser devtools, view-source, or the raw asset files on
# your CDN. Treat every VITE_ value as world-readable, permanently.
#
# NEVER put a secret, token, password, or full-privilege API key in a VITE_
# variable. Anything that must stay private belongs in a NON-prefixed variable
# (see REST_COUNTRIES_API_KEY below) or in your host's server-side runtime env.
#
# Copy this template to `.env` (git-ignored) and fill in real values locally.
# Never commit a filled-in copy.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# REST_COUNTRIES_API_KEY — BUILD-TIME ONLY, NOT PUBLIC
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The REST Countries API credential. Read ONLY by `npm run data:countries`
# (scripts/sync-countries.mjs), from your shell environment first and then
# from this git-ignored file, while refreshing public/data/countries.json.
#
# It carries NO `VITE_` prefix, so Vite never exposes it through
# `import.meta.env`. It is never inlined into the client bundle, never
# written into dist/, and never sent to the browser. Leave the value empty
# here and supply the real key from your shell or your CI secret store.
#
# Optional: the app ships the bundled dataset and needs no key at runtime.
REST_COUNTRIES_API_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# VITE_REST_COUNTRIES_KEY — PUBLIC BY DESIGN, RESTRICTED KEY ONLY
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional. The browser code still reads
# `import.meta.env.VITE_REST_COUNTRIES_KEY` (src/services/restCountries.ts,
# typed in src/vite-env.d.ts) and sends it as a Bearer token to the live
# REST Countries v5 API. So this value IS compiled into the shipped bundle
# and IS public.
#
# Rules for this variable:
#   - Leave it EMPTY unless you deliberately want opt-in live data.
#   - Only ever place a provider-issued PUBLIC / restricted-scope key here.
#   - NEVER reuse a full-privilege secret, an admin key, or any shared secret
#     in this variable. Once set, assume it is already public.
#   - The app works fully without it, using the bundled offline dataset
#     (public/data/countries.json, with 20 hand-curated fallbacks).
#
# `npm run data:countries` also accepts this name as a deprecated last-resort
# fallback, but prints a loud security warning and expects you to move the
# value to REST_COUNTRIES_API_KEY and rotate the exposed key.
VITE_REST_COUNTRIES_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SITE_URL / SITE — BUILD-TIME ONLY, NOT PUBLIC
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Required for a production deploy. Absolute origin (scheme + host, plus port
# if non-standard) with NO trailing slash.
# Example: https://global-explorer.example.com
#
# Read ONLY by `npm run sitemap` (scripts/build-sitemap.mjs), which prefers
# SITE and falls back to SITE_URL, or accepts a `--site=` argument. It writes
# public/sitemap.xml with absolute <loc> values and the matching `Sitemap:`
# line in public/robots.txt. Those are build-time files, so neither variable
# is inlined into the client bundle.
#
# `npm run build` does not read this itself — run `npm run sitemap` as its own
# step. Without a site URL the sitemap is written with relative <loc> values
# and the script warns.
SITE_URL=https://example.com

# Optional alias. Only consulted when SITE is set and SITE_URL is not.
SITE=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Credential hygiene
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# - Prefer a server-side / build-time credential (REST_COUNTRIES_API_KEY) over
#   anything exposed through a VITE_ variable.
# - Keep `.env` out of git. `.gitignore` already excludes `.env` and `.env.*`
#   and whitelists only `.env.example`.
# - Never paste a key into a shared channel, issue tracker, log, screenshot,
#   or commit message.
# - If a key has EVER appeared in a shared channel, a log, a commit, or a
#   client bundle, treat it as COMPROMISED: revoke it, issue a replacement,
#   and store the replacement in the server-side/CI secret store. Rotation is
#   the only fix; editing history or deleting a message does not help.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deploy checklist
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. cp .env.example .env    (PowerShell: Copy-Item .env.example .env)
# 2. Set SITE_URL to the real production origin (no trailing slash).
# 3. Export REST_COUNTRIES_API_KEY in the shell / CI secret store ONLY if you
#    intend to refresh the dataset with `npm run data:countries`. Leave
#    VITE_REST_COUNTRIES_KEY empty unless you accept shipping a public key.
# 4. npm ci                        (installs exactly what package-lock pins)
# 5. npm run check && npm test     (quality gates)
# 6. npm run build                 (tsc -b typecheck + vite build)
# 7. npm run sitemap               (regenerate sitemap.xml / robots.txt)
# 8. Deploy dist/ with the committed host config: netlify.toml, vercel.json,
#    public/_redirects, public/_headers, public/404.html,
#    public/staticwebapp.config.json (or public/.htaccess on Apache).
# 9. Sanity-check the built assets: no VITE_ value may be a real secret.